## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account security, least privilege, encryption, and auditability in a realistic data-lake setup.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles with MFA
- S3 Access Points for per-tenant isolation
- KMS key policies with rotating grants
- CloudTrail + AWS Config for compliance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Trust policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA_SCI_ACC:role/Tenant42DataScientist"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:MultiRoleSession": true}}
  }]
}
```

```javascript
// Permissions policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
    "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake-tenant42/*", "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake-tenant42"]
  },{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
    "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT:key/KEYID"
  }]
}
```

```javascript
// KMS key policy (JSON)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA_SCI_ACC:root"},
    "Action": ["kms:Decrypt"],
    "Resource": "*"
  }]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses advanced AWS ABAC, cross-account governance, and policy-as-code in a multi-tenant data lake; targets practical use of IAM Identity Center, PrincipalTags, Lake Formation, and a GitOps deployment flow.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC with session/principal tags
- Lake Formation fine-grained permissions
- Cross-account policy deployment (GitOps)
- Revocation latency and audits (CloudTrail, Config, Access Analyzer)

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:saml-provider/AWS_SSO" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-A/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "s3:prefix": "tenant-A/" }
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lakeformation:GetDataLocation", "lakeformation:GetTable", "lakeformation:GrantPermissions", "glue:GetDatabase", "glue:GetTable"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test revocation latency end-to-end? 
- How would you scale this across many tenants and regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of private AWS data access patterns, policy composition, and practical testing. 

## Key Concepts
- S3 public access blocking and bucket policy conditions
- VPC Endpoints and endpoint policies
- aws:SourceVpce condition and VPCE validation
- IAM roles for data producers

## Code Example
```
# Bucket policy (allow via VPCE)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-abc123"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```
# VPCE policy (minimal, allows basic access to bucket)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```
```
# IAM role for producer (trusts producer principal)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {"Effect": "Allow","Action": ["s3:PutObject"],"Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::landing-logs/*"}
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to multiple VPCEs per environment?
- How would you monitor and audit VPCE access over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bucket policies, per-prefix isolation, TLS enforcement, and practical test plans.

## Key Concepts
- S3 bucket policies, IAM roles, TLS enforcement, per-prefix access control
- Canary tests to verify allowed and denied actions

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamARole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamA/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamBRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamB/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs","arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/*"],
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a new team without duplicating policy blocks?
- How would you monitor and rotate the IAM roles used here?
